---
title: Quality Education Infrastructure
order: 5
icon: book-open
---

Across Nairobi County, thousands of children attend classes in dilapidated buildings with leaking roofs, cracked walls, and flooded pit latrines. In many public primary and secondary schools, classrooms designed for forty pupils hold seventy or eighty. Science laboratories are empty rooms with no equipment. Libraries are cupboards with a handful of outdated textbooks. This is the reality of our county's education infrastructure — and it is a betrayal of every child's constitutional right to education.

We demand a county-wide school infrastructure rehabilitation program that prioritizes the most urgent needs: classroom construction and renovation, modern science and computer laboratories, libraries stocked with current reference materials, and gender-sensitive sanitation facilities with running water. Every school must be accessible to learners with disabilities. We will establish a Schools Infrastructure Fund, financed through a dedicated allocation of at least 15 percent of the county development budget, with projects prioritized by a committee of education professionals and parent representatives.

For our brightest students from disadvantaged backgrounds, we demand a transparent, merit-based county scholarship program that covers tuition, books, and living expenses for secondary school and university. The selection process must be published online, with all awardees named and their schools disclosed. A county scholarship board, independent of political influence, will administer the program. No scholarship should be a reward for loyalty — it must be an investment in potential.

Nairobi's future depends on a skilled workforce. We demand the establishment of a vocational training center in every sub-county, offering accredited courses in information technology, electrical installation, plumbing, automotive engineering, hospitality, and the creative arts. Each center must partner with industry to ensure curricula are relevant and graduates are employable. We will also launch a county adult education program, giving every Nairobian who missed formal schooling a second chance to earn a basic certificate. Education is the surest path out of poverty — and the county government must pave it.
